  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="structural">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">📐 Structural Engineering</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is the difference between a simply supported beam and a fixed beam?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between a simply supported beam and a fixed beam? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>simply supported beam</strong> rests on two supports with pin and roller — it can rotate at both ends, so no end moments develop. Maximum moment = <span class="formula">M = wL²/8</span> at midspan for a UDL. A <strong>fixed beam</strong> has both ends fully restrained against rotation, generating hogging moments at supports. For UDL: end moment = wL²/12 and midspan moment = wL²/24 — roughly half the simply supported value. Fixed beams are stiffer and have less deflection (δ = wL⁴/384EI vs 5wL⁴/384EI), but require more complex connections and are sensitive to support settlement.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is bending moment and shear force? How are they related?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is bending moment and shear force? How are they related? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Shear Force (SF)</strong> at a section is the algebraic sum of all transverse forces to one side of that section. <strong>Bending Moment (BM)</strong> is the algebraic sum of moments of all forces about that section. Their key relationship: <span class="formula">dM/dx = V (shear) &nbsp;|&nbsp; dV/dx = −w (load intensity)</span> This means the slope of the BM diagram equals the SF, and shear is zero at the point of maximum bending moment. BMD and SFD are complementary diagrams used together in structural analysis and design.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="Explain the concept of neutral axis in beam design.">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain the concept of neutral axis in beam design. <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The <strong>neutral axis (NA)</strong> is the horizontal layer in a beam cross-section where there is zero longitudinal stress and zero strain under bending. Above the NA (in a sagging beam) the material is in compression; below it is in tension. For a homogeneous rectangular section, the NA passes through the centroid — the mid-depth. For composite sections (e.g., RC beam), the NA shifts toward the stiffer/stronger material. The NA depth is found by equating compressive and tensile forces, which is fundamental to calculating moment capacity and reinforcement requirements.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is the difference between working stress design and limit state design?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between working stress design (WSD) and limit state design (LSD)? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Working Stress Design (WSD / Elastic Design):</strong> Stresses under service (unfactored) loads must not exceed allowable stresses = material strength ÷ factor of safety. Simple but overly conservative as it doesn't account for actual failure modes.<br><br><strong>Limit State Design (LSD):</strong> Structures are designed to not reach defined limit states. Uses factored loads (ULS: Ultimate Limit State) and checks serviceability (SLS: deflection, cracking). Partial factors applied to both loads (γf) and materials (γm). <span class="formula">Design effect ≤ Design resistance: γf·Qk ≤ Rk/γm</span> LSD is more rational, allows better material utilization, and is the basis of Eurocodes, ACI 318, and BS 8110.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is the effective length of a column and why does it matter?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is effective length of a column and why does it matter? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The <strong>effective length (Le)</strong> is the equivalent pin-ended length that has the same buckling load as the actual column with its end conditions. <span class="formula">Le = k·L &nbsp;|&nbsp; k = 0.5 (fixed-fixed), 0.7 (fixed-pin), 1.0 (pin-pin), 2.0 (fixed-free)</span> It matters because buckling (Euler) load is inversely proportional to Le²: <span class="formula">P_cr = π²EI / Le²</span> A fixed-fixed column can carry 4× the load of a pin-pin column of the same length. The slenderness ratio λ = Le/r determines whether a column is short (crushing governs) or slender (buckling governs).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is the purpose of reinforcement in a concrete beam?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the purpose of reinforcement in a concrete beam? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Concrete is strong in compression (~25–50 MPa) but weak in tension (~1/10th of compressive strength). In a loaded beam the tension zone (bottom of a sagging beam) would crack and fail without reinforcement. <strong>Main steel bars</strong> carry tensile forces in the tension zone. <strong>Shear links/stirrups</strong> resist diagonal tension from shear forces. <strong>Compression reinforcement</strong> may be added to enhance ductility or reduce long-term deflection. <strong>Minimum steel</strong> (As,min) controls crack widths and ensures some post-cracking strength. The combination creates a ductile, crack-controlled member capable of both bending and shear resistance.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is moment redistribution in RC design?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is moment redistribution in RC design? <span class="iv-tag adv">Advanced</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Moment redistribution</strong> is the transfer of bending moment from a highly stressed section (typically at supports) to less stressed sections (midspan) in a continuous beam/frame. It relies on plastic hinge rotation at the critical section before collapse. This allows the designer to reduce support moments and increase span moments, leading to more economical reinforcement layouts. In Eurocode 2, redistribution up to 30% is permitted for class B/C steel, subject to the neutral axis depth condition: <span class="formula">x/d ≤ (δ − 0.44) / 1.25</span> where δ is the redistribution ratio. This ensures adequate ductility so the hinge can rotate sufficiently before failure.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="Explain the difference between one-way and two-way slabs.">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain the difference between one-way and two-way slabs. <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The distinction depends on the aspect ratio (Ly/Lx): <strong>One-way slab:</strong> Ly/Lx &gt; 2 — load is transferred primarily in the short direction. Main reinforcement runs in one direction; secondary/distribution bars run in the other. <strong>Two-way slab:</strong> Ly/Lx ≤ 2 — load is shared in both directions. Main reinforcement required in both directions. More efficient material use for square panels. Two-way slabs have significantly lower deflections and moments compared to one-way slabs of similar spans because the load is shared. Design uses BS 8110 coefficient tables or yield line theory for irregular panels.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is punching shear and how is it checked?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is punching shear and how is it checked? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Punching shear</strong> occurs when a concentrated load (e.g., column) tries to punch through a flat slab. It's a 3D shear failure on a truncated cone around the column. Check involves: <span class="formula">v_Ed = V_Ed / (u₀·d) ≤ v_Rd,c</span> where u₀ is the perimeter at 2d from column face (Eurocode) and v_Rd,c is the concrete shear resistance. If v_Ed exceeds capacity, shear reinforcement (studs, bent-down bars) is required, or the slab must be thickened (drop panel/column head). Punching shear is critical in flat plate/flat slab construction without beams.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What is the plastic moment capacity and plastic hinge?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is plastic moment capacity and a plastic hinge? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The <strong>plastic moment (Mp)</strong> is the moment at which the entire cross-section has yielded, forming a <strong>plastic hinge</strong> — a section that can rotate indefinitely at constant moment. <span class="formula">Mp = fy · Zp &nbsp;|&nbsp; where Zp = plastic section modulus</span> The ratio Mp/Me = Zp/Ze = shape factor (≈1.15 for I-sections, 1.5 for rectangles). A structure forms a collapse mechanism when sufficient plastic hinges develop. Plastic design allows redistribution of moments in statically indeterminate structures, giving more economical designs than elastic methods. Ductility of steel makes plastic design valid; RC uses similar principles in limit state design.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="What are the differences between braced and unbraced frames?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are the differences between braced and unbraced frames? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>braced frame</strong> has a separate lateral load-resisting system (core walls, shear walls, diagonal bracing) — columns carry primarily axial loads and vertical actions only. Effective length factor k ≤ 1.0. Sway is prevented by the bracing system. An <strong>unbraced (sway) frame</strong> relies on beam-column connections and frame action to resist lateral loads. Effective length k &gt; 1.0, increasing column slenderness and requiring second-order (P-Δ) analysis for significant sway. Unbraced frames are more flexible but architecturally open; braced frames are stiffer and more efficient for tall buildings.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="structural" data-q="Explain Euler's buckling theory and its limitations.">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain Euler's buckling theory and its limitations. <span class="iv-tag adv">Advanced</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Euler's theory gives the critical axial load for a slender column to buckle: <span class="formula">P_cr = π²EI / Le²</span> <strong>Assumptions:</strong> perfectly straight, homogeneous elastic material, axially loaded, small deflections. <strong>Limitations:</strong> (1) Real columns have initial imperfections and eccentricity — actual buckling loads are lower. (2) Only valid for slender columns (high λ); short/intermediate columns fail by crushing or inelastic buckling. (3) Doesn't account for residual stresses in steel sections. (4) Assumes uniform cross-section. In practice, design codes (Eurocode 3, AISC) use column curves that account for initial imperfections via reduction factor χ applied to the squash load.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

