  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="geotech">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">🌍 Geotechnical Engineering &amp; Soil Mechanics</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is the difference between cohesive and non-cohesive soils?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between cohesive and non-cohesive soils? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Cohesive soils</strong> (clays, silts) derive shear strength primarily from cohesion (interparticle attraction, clay mineralogy). Shear strength: <span class="formula">τ = c + σ·tan(φ) &nbsp;|&nbsp; c &gt; 0, φ may be small or zero</span> Prone to consolidation settlement, swelling, and time-dependent behaviour. Sensitive to moisture changes. <strong>Non-cohesive soils</strong> (sands, gravels) have zero or negligible cohesion — strength depends entirely on friction angle φ and normal stress. <span class="formula">τ = σ·tan(φ) &nbsp;|&nbsp; c = 0</span> Drain quickly, settle rapidly under load, do not exhibit consolidation. Easy to compact. The USCS classifies soils using grain size, Atterberg limits, and gradation to distinguish clay (CL, CH), silt (ML, MH), sand (SP, SW), and gravel (GP, GW).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is consolidation settlement and how does it differ from elastic settlement?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is consolidation settlement and how does it differ from elastic settlement? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Elastic (immediate) settlement</strong> occurs rapidly upon loading as soil deforms elastically. Calculated using elasticity theory: <span class="formula">Si = q·B·(1−ν²)·Iw / Es</span> Relevant for sands and gravels that drain fast. <strong>Consolidation settlement</strong> occurs in clay as excess pore water pressure slowly dissipates under sustained loading, causing volume decrease. Primary consolidation: <span class="formula">Sc = Cc·H/(1+e₀)·log[(σ'₀+Δσ)/σ'₀]</span> Time-dependent — may take years/decades in thick clay. Secondary consolidation (creep) follows primary. Total settlement = elastic + primary consolidation + secondary. This distinction drives foundation design in clay-dominated profiles.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is the Terzaghi bearing capacity equation?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the Terzaghi bearing capacity equation and what are the Nc, Nq, Nγ factors? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Terzaghi's equation gives the ultimate bearing capacity of a shallow foundation: <span class="formula">q_u = c·Nc + γ·D·Nq + 0.5·γ·B·Nγ</span> <strong>Nc</strong> = cohesion factor (from c·Nc) — dominates in undrained clays. <strong>Nq</strong> = surcharge factor — accounts for overburden pressure at foundation depth. <strong>Nγ</strong> = self-weight factor — accounts for the wedge of soil below the foundation. For strip: q_u = c·Nc + qNq + 0.5γBNγ; for square: 1.3c·Nc + qNq + 0.4γBNγ; for circle: 1.3c·Nc + qNq + 0.3γBNγ. The allowable bearing capacity = q_u / FOS (typically FOS=3). These factors depend on φ and are tabulated or calculated from: <span class="formula">Nq = e^(π·tanφ)·tan²(45+φ/2) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Nc = (Nq−1)/tanφ</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What are Atterberg limits and what do they tell us?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are Atterberg limits and what do they tell us about a soil? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Atterberg limits describe the moisture content boundaries between different states of fine-grained soils. <strong>Shrinkage Limit (SL):</strong> MC below which soil doesn't shrink further. <strong>Plastic Limit (PL):</strong> minimum MC at which soil can be rolled into a 3mm thread. <strong>Liquid Limit (LL):</strong> MC at which soil transitions from plastic to liquid state. <strong>Plasticity Index (PI) = LL − PL</strong> — higher PI means more plastic/expansive. <strong>Liquidity Index (LI) = (w−PL)/PI</strong> — if LI &gt; 1 soil is in liquid state; if LI &lt; 0, soil is in solid state. These limits classify soils on the Casagrande plasticity chart and predict compressibility, shrink-swell behaviour, and suitability as fill.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is an active and passive earth pressure? How is Ka calculated?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is active vs passive earth pressure? How are Ka and Kp calculated? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Active pressure</strong> develops when a wall moves away from soil — soil expands laterally and shear failure occurs. Minimum pressure state. <strong>Passive pressure</strong> develops when a wall is pushed into soil — soil is compressed. Maximum resistance. <span class="formula">Ka = tan²(45° − φ/2) = (1−sinφ)/(1+sinφ)</span><span class="formula">Kp = tan²(45° + φ/2) = 1/Ka</span>Rankine's active pressure: <span class="formula">σ_a = Ka·γ·z − 2c·√Ka</span> At-rest pressure (no movement): K₀ = 1−sinφ (Jaky's formula). Active pressure is used for retaining wall design; passive pressure provides resistance at the toe. Typical Ka for dense sand ≈ 0.28, Kp ≈ 3.5.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is the factor of safety against slope failure?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">How do you assess slope stability and what is an acceptable FOS? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Slope stability is assessed by comparing the resisting shear strength along a failure surface to the driving shear stress. <span class="formula">FOS = Resisting moment / Overturning moment = Σ(c'l + N'tanφ') / ΣT</span> Methods include: <strong>Ordinary/Fellenius method:</strong> ignores interslice forces — conservative. <strong>Bishop simplified:</strong> accounts for vertical interslice forces — more accurate. <strong>Morgenstern-Price / Spencer:</strong> fully rigorous. FOS acceptance: permanent slopes — FOS ≥ 1.5; temporary slopes — FOS ≥ 1.25; critical infrastructure — FOS ≥ 1.5–2.0. Key triggers for failure: increased pore water pressure (rainfall, flood), toe erosion, loading at crest, seismic shaking, and vegetation removal.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is the difference between SPT and CPT site investigation tests?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between SPT and CPT in site investigation? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Standard Penetration Test (SPT):</strong> A split-spoon sampler is driven 300mm into the borehole floor using a 63.5kg hammer falling 760mm; N-value = blows for last 300mm. Provides: soil profile, N-values for bearing capacity and pile design. Disturbed sample recovered. Simple, widely used globally. <strong>Cone Penetration Test (CPT):</strong> A cone probe is pushed continuously at 20mm/s; measures tip resistance (qc) and sleeve friction (fs) continuously. No sample, but provides very detailed stratigraphic profile. CPTU also measures pore pressure (u2). CPT is more repeatable, faster, and provides continuous data; SPT is better in gravels and for sample recovery. Both are used together on most site investigations.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is liquefaction and when does it occur?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is soil liquefaction and when does it occur? <span class="iv-tag adv">Advanced</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Liquefaction</strong> is the phenomenon where saturated, loose cohesionless soil (sand/silt) temporarily loses shear strength and behaves like a liquid under cyclic loading (earthquake, vibration). Mechanism: cyclic stress → excess pore pressure buildup → effective stress → 0 → shear strength → 0. Conditions favourable for liquefaction: loose to medium dense sand, high water table (soil saturated), cyclic loading (seismic), uniform grain size. Assessment: Simplified Seed-Idriss method compares Cyclic Stress Ratio (CSR) to Cyclic Resistance Ratio (CRR) from SPT N-values. Mitigation: densification (vibrocompaction), drainage, grouting, replacement, deep foundations through liquefiable layer.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="geotech" data-q="What is the difference between shallow and deep foundations?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between shallow and deep foundations? When is each used? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Shallow foundations</strong> (Df/B ≤ 1–5) — pad, strip, raft — transfer load to near-surface soil through bearing pressure. Used when adequate bearing capacity exists within 2–3m of surface. Simple and economical. <strong>Deep foundations</strong> (Df/B &gt; 5) — piles, caissons, piers — transfer load to deeper, stronger strata through skin friction (shaft resistance) and/or end bearing. Used when: (1) surface soils are weak/compressible, (2) heavy structural loads, (3) risk of scour/erosion, (4) differential settlement must be minimised, (5) structure on slope or near excavation. Pile types: bored (CFA, rotary), driven (precast, steel H), micro-piles. Total pile capacity = end bearing + skin friction, with FOS typically 2.5–3.0.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── HYDRAULICS ── -->
  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="hydraulics">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">💧 Hydraulics &amp; Hydrology</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is Manning's equation and what is the roughness coefficient?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is Manning's equation and what does the roughness coefficient n represent? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Manning's equation gives the average velocity in an open channel: <span class="formula">V = (1/n)·R^(2/3)·S^(1/2) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Q = A·V</span> <strong>n</strong> = Manning's roughness coefficient (dimensionless) — represents resistance to flow from channel surface texture. Typical values: concrete pipe n≈0.013, natural stream n≈0.03–0.07, grass channel n≈0.025–0.05. <strong>R = A/P</strong> (hydraulic radius = area/wetted perimeter). <strong>S</strong> = channel slope (m/m). Manning's is empirical, valid for fully turbulent flow, and widely used for design of drains, pipes, and natural channels. It's limited for laminar flow and does not account for rapidly varied flow conditions.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is the Darcy-Weisbach equation for pipe flow?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain the Darcy-Weisbach equation for pipe head loss. <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The Darcy-Weisbach equation calculates head loss due to friction in a pipe: <span class="formula">h_f = f·(L/D)·(V²/2g)</span> where f = Darcy friction factor, L = pipe length (m), D = diameter (m), V = velocity (m/s). The friction factor f depends on flow regime: laminar (Re &lt; 2300): f = 64/Re; turbulent (Re &gt; 4000): use Moody chart or Colebrook-White equation. Compared to Hazen-Williams (empirical, water only), Darcy-Weisbach is more fundamental and applicable to any fluid. Head loss = energy lost per unit weight — it reduces the hydraulic grade line. Minor losses (bends, valves) are added as: <span class="formula">h_minor = K·V²/2g</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is the Froude number and what does it indicate?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the Froude number and what flow regimes does it define? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The Froude number is a dimensionless ratio of inertial to gravitational forces in open channel flow: <span class="formula">Fr = V / √(g·y) &nbsp;|&nbsp; V = flow velocity, y = depth</span> <strong>Fr &lt; 1:</strong> Subcritical (tranquil) flow — slow, deep. Gravity dominates. Downstream control. <strong>Fr = 1:</strong> Critical flow — minimum specific energy for a given discharge. <strong>Fr &gt; 1:</strong> Supercritical (rapid/shooting) flow — fast, shallow. Inertia dominates. Upstream control. A <strong>hydraulic jump</strong> occurs when supercritical flow transitions to subcritical, dissipating energy — important in spillway design and stilling basins. Critical depth: <span class="formula">y_c = (q²/g)^(1/3)</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is the rational method for stormwater runoff?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the Rational Method for stormwater runoff estimation? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The Rational Method is the most widely used formula for peak stormwater discharge from small catchments (&lt;50 ha): <span class="formula">Q = C·i·A / 360 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Q in m³/s, i in mm/hr, A in ha</span> <strong>C</strong> = runoff coefficient (0.9 impervious, 0.3 grass, 0.7 urban). <strong>i</strong> = rainfall intensity for storm duration = time of concentration tc (from IDF curves). <strong>A</strong> = catchment area. Assumptions: uniform rainfall, steady-state conditions, storm duration = tc. Limitations: oversimplifies for large/complex catchments — use SCS-CN or hydrological routing methods for larger areas. The method is quick, transparent, and acceptable for pipe drainage design, culverts, and small retention basins.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is specific energy in open channel flow?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is specific energy in open channel flow and why is it important? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Specific energy (E) is the energy per unit weight relative to the channel bed: <span class="formula">E = y + V²/(2g) = y + Q²/(2gA²)</span> where y = depth, V²/2g = velocity head. For a given discharge, the E-y curve shows two possible depths for any E &gt; E_min: the subcritical (deeper) and supercritical (shallower) conjugate depths. At critical depth E is minimum: <span class="formula">E_min = (3/2)·y_c (rectangular channel)</span> Specific energy is essential for: designing transitions (contractions, expansions), flumes, weirs, and understanding backwater effects. A channel narrowing or bed rise reduces specific energy, potentially causing critical conditions and choking the flow upstream.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is the SCS Curve Number method?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain the SCS Curve Number (CN) method for estimating runoff. <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The SCS-CN (now NRCS-CN) method estimates direct runoff from a storm event: <span class="formula">S = (25400/CN) − 254 &nbsp;|&nbsp; (S in mm, maximum potential retention)</span><span class="formula">Q = (P − 0.2S)² / (P + 0.8S) &nbsp;if P &gt; 0.2S, else Q=0</span> CN ranges 0–100: CN=98 = impervious, CN=55 = meadow, CN=80 = residential. CN depends on land use, soil hydrological group (A–D), and antecedent moisture condition (AMC I–III). Advantages over Rational method: accounts for soil saturation (AMC), storm volume rather than just peak rate, applicable to medium catchments. Widely used in North America, increasingly internationally.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="hydraulics" data-q="What is Bernoulli's equation and where does it apply in civil engineering?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is Bernoulli's equation and its applications in civil engineering? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Bernoulli's equation expresses conservation of energy for ideal (inviscid) steady flow along a streamline: <span class="formula">p/γ + V²/2g + z = constant (Total Head)</span> where p/γ = pressure head, V²/2g = velocity head, z = elevation head. Civil engineering applications: (1) Flow measurement — Venturi meters, orifice plates, Pitot tubes. (2) Design of pipe systems — pressure variation through networks. (3) Weir and spillway flow analysis. (4) Nozzle discharge and culvert flow. (5) Groundwater flow (Darcy's Law is Bernoulli modified for porous media). In practice, energy losses (friction, minor losses) are added as: <span class="formula">H₁ = H₂ + h_L</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

