  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="materials">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">🔵 Materials — Concrete, Steel &amp; Masonry</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What is water-cement ratio and why is it important?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the water-cement (w/c) ratio and why is it so important? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The <strong>w/c ratio</strong> = mass of water / mass of cement in a concrete mix. It directly controls: <strong>Strength:</strong> Lower w/c → higher strength. Abrams' Law: <span class="formula">fc = A / B^(w/c) &nbsp;|&nbsp; A,B are empirical constants</span> Typical: w/c=0.4 → ~40 MPa; w/c=0.6 → ~25 MPa. <strong>Durability:</strong> Lower w/c → less permeable → better resistance to chloride ingress, carbonation, freeze-thaw. <strong>Workability:</strong> Higher w/c → more fluid → easier placing but weaker. The minimum water needed for hydration ≈ w/c=0.22; excess water creates capillary pores on drying. Codes specify maximum w/c ratios for exposure classes: XC1→0.65, XD2→0.50, XS3→0.45 (Eurocode 2).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What is the difference between ordinary Portland cement and blended cements?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between OPC and blended/supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs)? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>OPC (CEM I):</strong> Ground Portland clinker — high early strength, standard durability, highest embodied carbon (~810 kg CO₂/tonne). <strong>Blended cements / SCMs:</strong> Replace part of OPC clinker: <strong>GGBS (Ground Granulated Blast Slag):</strong> Up to 70% replacement — slower strength gain, improved sulfate resistance, reduced heat of hydration, lower permeability. Good for mass concrete and marine exposure. <strong>PFA/Fly Ash:</strong> 25–35% replacement — reduces heat, improves workability, lower cost. Long-term strength gain. <strong>Silica Fume:</strong> 5–10% — ultra-fine, highly reactive, significantly reduces permeability and improves strength. Used in HPC/UHPC. Environmental drivers are pushing toward higher SCM contents to reduce the ~8% of global CO₂ from cement production.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What is concrete carbonation and how does it affect durability?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is carbonation in concrete and why does it matter? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Carbonation</strong> is the reaction of atmospheric CO₂ with calcium hydroxide in the cement paste: <span class="formula">Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O</span> This reduces concrete pH from ~13 to below 9, destroying the passive oxide film that protects steel reinforcement. Once the carbonation front reaches the rebar, corrosion begins in the presence of moisture and oxygen. Carbonation depth follows: <span class="formula">d = k·√t &nbsp;|&nbsp; k = carbonation coefficient</span> Factors affecting k: w/c ratio (lower = slower), cement type (OPC slower than GGBS), curing quality, exposure conditions. Prevention: adequate cover (≥25mm for internal; ≥35mm exposed), low w/c, good curing, protective coatings, carbonation-resistant surface treatments.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What is the difference between yield strength and tensile strength in steel?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between yield strength, tensile strength and ductility in steel? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Yield strength (fy):</strong> The stress at which permanent plastic deformation begins — steel no longer returns to original length. For structural steel S355: fy = 355 MPa. Design is generally based on yield strength. <strong>Tensile strength (fu):</strong> Maximum stress before fracture — the peak of the engineering stress-strain curve. For S355: fu = 490–630 MPa. The ratio fu/fy indicates strain hardening capacity. <strong>Ductility</strong> is the ability to undergo large plastic deformation before fracture — measured by elongation at break (typically 15–25% for structural steel). High ductility is essential for: seismic design (energy dissipation), plastic design (hinge rotation), connection robustness, and preventing brittle fracture. Steel's linear elastic range, yield plateau, strain hardening, and ductile fracture make it ideal for structural use.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What causes concrete cracking and how can it be controlled?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are the main causes of concrete cracking and how is it controlled? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Pre-hardening causes:</strong> Plastic shrinkage (rapid surface drying), plastic settlement (differential settlement around reinforcement), formwork movement. <strong>Post-hardening causes:</strong> Drying shrinkage (most common — moisture loss causes contraction), thermal effects (heat of hydration in mass concrete, freeze-thaw cycling), structural loading (flexural tension cracks, shear cracks), AAR (Alkali-Aggregate Reaction — expansive gel), corrosion of reinforcement (expansive rust), sulphate attack. <strong>Control measures:</strong> Adequate curing, low w/c, proper mix design, closely spaced reinforcement (small bars), expansion/contraction joints, fibre reinforcement, early thermal monitoring in mass concrete, appropriate cover and concrete specification for durability. Crack width limits: w_max = 0.3mm (reinforced), 0.2mm (prestressed) per Eurocode 2.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What are the grades of structural steel and their typical applications?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are the common structural steel grades and their applications? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Structural steels are designated by yield strength (EN 10025): <strong>S235:</strong> fy=235 MPa — light structures, secondary members, purlins. <strong>S275:</strong> fy=275 MPa — general use, traditional UK standard (formerly Grade 43). <strong>S355:</strong> fy=355 MPa — most common UK/European structural grade — beams, columns, bridges. <strong>S420/S460:</strong> High-strength — heavy offshore, long-span bridges, reducing weight. Sub-grades (J0, J2, K2) indicate Charpy impact test temperature for fracture toughness. For seismic design, Class 1 and 2 cross-sections are required for ductility. ASTM grades (USA): A36 (fy=250 MPa), A572 Gr50 (fy=345 MPa ≈ S355), A992 for wide-flange sections.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="materials" data-q="What is prestressed concrete and how does it work?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is prestressed concrete and what are its advantages? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Prestressed concrete</strong> has high-tensile steel tendons (strands, wires, or bars) deliberately tensioned to put the concrete into compression before service loads are applied. The induced compressive stress counteracts tensile stresses from loading, preventing or controlling cracking. <strong>Pre-tensioning:</strong> Tendons tensioned before casting, released after curing — bond transfers prestress. Used for factory-produced elements (beams, hollowcore slabs). <strong>Post-tensioning:</strong> Tendons in ducts tensioned after casting and grouted. Used in-situ for bridges, transfer slabs, parking structures. <strong>Advantages vs RC:</strong> Thinner, lighter sections; spans up to 3× longer; crack-free under service loads; better fatigue resistance; reduced deflection. Requires careful detailing of anchorage zones, losses (elastic shortening, creep, shrinkage, relaxation), and prestress loss calculations.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

