  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="roads">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">🛣️ Road, Transport &amp; Survey Engineering</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is stopping sight distance and how is it calculated?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is stopping sight distance (SSD) and how is it calculated? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">SSD is the minimum distance a driver needs to see an obstruction and bring a vehicle to a complete stop safely. It has two components: <span class="formula">SSD = d_r + d_b = V·t/3.6 + V²/[254·(f+G)]</span> <strong>d_r</strong> = reaction distance: V (km/h) × perception-reaction time (typically 2.5s). <strong>d_b</strong> = braking distance: depends on speed, coefficient of friction f (0.28–0.40 depending on road surface), and grade G (+ downhill). SSD is the minimum design control for all roads — horizontal and vertical geometry must provide at least SSD to the driver's eye height (1.05m) over an object height of 0.2m (kerb). Typical SSD values: 30km/h → 30m; 80km/h → 120m; 120km/h → 280m.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is the difference between flexible and rigid pavements?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between flexible and rigid pavements? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Flexible pavement</strong> (asphalt): Multilayer system — wearing course, binder course, base, sub-base. Load is distributed through layers by grain interlock; subgrade bears significant stress. Designed using CBR/AASHTO method — thickness depends on traffic (ESAs) and subgrade strength. Fails by rutting, fatigue cracking, and potholing. Maintenance by overlay. <strong>Rigid pavement</strong> (concrete): Portland cement concrete slab (150–300mm). Slab bending resistance distributes load over large area — subgrade stress is low. Joints (expansion/contraction) control cracking. Longer life (20–40 years), higher initial cost. Fails by slab cracking, joint failure, pumping. Suitable for heavy loading (ports, airports) and poor subgrades. Designed using PCA or Westergaard methods for slab thickness.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is a transition curve and why is it used?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is a transition curve (spiral) and why is it used in road design? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>transition curve</strong> (clothoid/Euler spiral) is a curve of gradually varying radius inserted between a straight and a circular curve. Its radius decreases from infinity (straight) to R (circular). Purpose: (1) <strong>Gradual introduction of centrifugal force</strong> — prevents sudden lateral shock on vehicles entering a curve at speed. (2) <strong>Superelevation runoff</strong> — the road cross-fall is gradually rotated from normal camber to full superelevation over the transition length. (3) <strong>Aesthetics</strong> — smoother appearance. The standard clothoid relationship: <span class="formula">R·L = A² (constant) &nbsp;|&nbsp; L = transition length, R = radius</span> Minimum transition length is governed by rate of change of centrifugal acceleration (DMRB: &lt;0.3–0.6 m/s³) and minimum superelevation runoff length.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is the CBR test and how is it used in pavement design?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the CBR test and how is it used in pavement design? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The <strong>California Bearing Ratio (CBR)</strong> test measures the resistance of a soil to penetration by a standard plunger at a rate of 1.27mm/min. <span class="formula">CBR (%) = (test load / standard load) × 100</span> Standard loads: 13.2 kN at 2.5mm, 20.0 kN at 5.0mm. CBR is used to assess subgrade (and sub-base) strength for pavement design. Typical values: very poor subgrade CBR ≤ 2%, good subgrade CBR &gt; 10–15%. In the TRRL/TRL method (UK), CBR is used with design traffic (msa) to select pavement thickness from design charts. In AASHTO method, CBR is converted to resilient modulus: <span class="formula">Mr (MPa) ≈ 10 × CBR (Heukelom &amp; Klomp)</span> Higher CBR → thinner required pavement, so improving subgrade (drainage, stabilisation) significantly reduces pavement cost.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is a vertical curve in highway design and how is the K-value used?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is a vertical curve and how is the K-value used? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A vertical curve is a parabolic curve connecting two road gradients (g1 and g2) at a summit or sag. It provides a smooth transition in the vertical profile. <span class="formula">L = K·A &nbsp;|&nbsp; A = |g1−g2| (%), K = rate of vertical curvature</span> <strong>Summit curves:</strong> K determined by SSD (sight distance over crest). Higher design speed → higher K → longer curve. <strong>Sag curves:</strong> K determined by headlight sight distance (night driving) or comfort (vertical acceleration). The <strong>K-value</strong> (from DMRB / AASHTO) directly gives required curve length per 1% change in grade. Example: 85km/h design speed, summit K=100 → A=3% → L=300m. Minimum K ensures adequate sight distance and passenger comfort (vertical acceleration &lt; 0.05g for sag curves).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="roads" data-q="What is differential levelling and how is it performed?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain differential levelling and the height of collimation method. <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Differential levelling determines the difference in elevation between points using a level and staff. <strong>Height of Collimation (HPC) method:</strong> <span class="formula">HPC = RL(datum) + Back Sight &nbsp;|&nbsp; RL(point) = HPC − Foresight/Intermediate Sight</span> Procedure: Set up level between known point (BM) and unknown point. Take backsight (BS) on BM, intermediate sights (IS) on any points, foresight (FS) when moving instrument. Each instrument position gives one HPC. Arithmetic check: <span class="formula">ΣBS − ΣFS = Last RL − First RL</span> Closing error (misclose) should be within: <span class="formula">Allowable misclose = ±5√n mm (n = number of setups)</span> Used for road levelling, drainage gradient setting, building setting out, volume calculations, and topographic surveys.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

