  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="construction">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">🏗️ Construction Methods &amp; Site Engineering</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What is the critical path method (CPM) in construction planning?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the Critical Path Method (CPM) in construction planning? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">CPM is a network-based project scheduling technique that identifies the longest path through a project — the <strong>critical path</strong> — which determines the minimum project duration. Activities on the critical path have zero float (slack). <strong>Key terms:</strong> ES (Early Start), EF (Early Finish) from forward pass; LS (Late Start), LF (Late Finish) from backward pass. <span class="formula">Total Float = LS − ES = LF − EF</span> Activities with TF=0 are critical — any delay extends the project. Benefits: identifies critical activities requiring close monitoring, enables resource levelling, supports 'what if' analysis, basis for contract delay claims. CPM is complemented by the Gantt chart for visual communication. Tools: Primavera P6, MS Project.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What are the different types of piling methods?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Describe different types of piling methods and when each is used. <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Driven piles:</strong> Steel H-piles, precast concrete, timber — hammered into ground. Fast, good quality control. Noise/vibration concerns in urban areas. Good for sandy and granular soils. <strong>Bored piles (rotary/auger):</strong> CFA (Continuous Flight Auger) — quick, low noise, good in weak soils. Rotary bored piles — for large diameters and hard strata. Risk of base softening in clays. <strong>Mini-piles / micro-piles:</strong> Small diameter (&lt;300mm), high grout-to-steel ratio — used in restricted access, underpinning, and poor access conditions. <strong>Screw piles:</strong> Steel helix screwed in — quick, no spoil, removable, good for temporary works. <strong>Selection criteria:</strong> Ground conditions, load magnitude, proximity to existing structures, noise constraints, programme, cost, and required pile capacity. Pile testing (static load test, PDA) verifies capacity.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What is falsework and formwork in concrete construction?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between formwork and falsework? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Formwork</strong> is the temporary mould into which concrete is poured to give it its required shape — it is in direct contact with the concrete. Materials: timber, steel, aluminium, plastic, GRP. Must be watertight, strong enough to resist fluid concrete pressure, and provide required finish. <strong>Falsework</strong> is the temporary support structure that carries the weight of formwork and wet concrete until it gains sufficient strength — it is the supporting framework (props, frames, towers) but not in direct contact with concrete. Falsework is classified by risk (BS 5975): Class 1 (routine), Class 2 (complex), Class 3 (critical). <strong>Striking time</strong> (when to remove): depends on concrete strength gain, element type, and minimum temperature. Too early → deflection/cracking; too late → delays. Table A in BS 8110 or CIRIA C660 guidance.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What is the purpose of a method statement and risk assessment?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is a Method Statement and Risk Assessment in construction? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>Method Statement</strong> describes in detail how a specific activity will be carried out — sequence of operations, plant and equipment, materials, supervision, and safety measures. Required for complex/hazardous operations under CDM 2015 (UK) and similar regulations. A <strong>Risk Assessment</strong> identifies hazards associated with an activity, assesses the likelihood and severity of harm, and specifies control measures. <span class="formula">Risk = Likelihood × Severity (consequence)</span> Controls follow the hierarchy: Eliminate → Substitute → Engineering controls → Administrative controls → PPE. Together, method statements and risk assessments form the basis of a <strong>RAMS (Risk Assessment and Method Statement)</strong> document — required by principal contractors, reviewed by H&S advisors, and signed off by operatives before work commences.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What is compaction and why is it important in earthworks?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is compaction in earthworks and why is it important? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Compaction</strong> is the mechanical densification of soil by applying energy (rolling, vibrating, tamping) to expel air voids — NOT water. It increases dry density, shear strength, bearing capacity, and reduces settlement and permeability. The <strong>Standard Proctor Test</strong> gives the relationship between dry density and moisture content, identifying: Optimum Moisture Content (OMC) — maximum dry density point. Field compaction should achieve ≥95% of maximum dry density (Modified Proctor for highways). Quality control: Nuclear Density Meter, Sand Replacement Test, or Dynamic Cone Penetrometer (DCP). Compaction equipment selection depends on soil type: vibratory rollers for sands/gravels; sheepsfoot/padfoot rollers for clays. Layer thickness typically 150–300mm. Over-compaction at high moisture content causes remoulding and strength reduction in clays.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="construction" data-q="What is the purpose of a CDM coordinator/principal designer?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is CDM 2015 and the role of the Principal Designer? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>CDM 2015</strong> (Construction Design and Management Regulations 2015, UK) manages health and safety in construction projects. It requires: <strong>Client:</strong> appoints principal designer and principal contractor, ensures suitable arrangements. <strong>Principal Designer:</strong> Plans, manages, monitors, and coordinates pre-construction phase H&S. Produces pre-construction information pack. Prepares Health and Safety File. Ensures design hazards are eliminated/mitigated. <strong>Principal Contractor:</strong> Plans and manages construction phase H&S. Produces Construction Phase Plan. Controls site. CDM applies to ALL construction projects in the UK — notification to HSE (F10) required for projects lasting &gt;30 days with 20+ workers simultaneously, or exceeding 500 person-days. CDM aims to integrate H&S into design and planning stages, not just manage it on site reactively.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── MANAGEMENT ── -->
  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="management">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">📊 Project &amp; Contract Management</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="management" data-q="What is the difference between NEC and JCT contracts?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between NEC and JCT contracts? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>NEC (New Engineering Contract / NEC4):</strong> Collaborative, process-driven contract used extensively in UK public sector (HS2, Highways England, Network Rail). Uses Early Warning Notices (EWNs), compensation events, and a Programme (Clause 31/32). Payment options A–F (lump sum, target cost, cost reimbursable, etc.). Promotes early issue resolution and joint risk management. <strong>JCT (Joint Contracts Tribunal):</strong> Traditional adversarial contract — widely used in private building sector. Various forms: SBC (Standard Building Contract), D&B (Design and Build), Minor Works. Claims-based dispute resolution. JCT is more familiar to contractors for building work; NEC favoured for civil infrastructure. Both can include BIM requirements, sustainability targets, and incentive mechanisms in their contract data. FIDIC is the international equivalent used for overseas projects.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="management" data-q="What is earned value management (EVM)?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is Earned Value Management (EVM) and what are the key metrics? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">EVM integrates scope, schedule, and cost to objectively measure project performance: <strong>PV (Planned Value):</strong> Budgeted cost of work scheduled. <strong>EV (Earned Value):</strong> Budgeted cost of work actually performed. <strong>AC (Actual Cost):</strong> Actual cost incurred. <span class="formula">SV = EV−PV (Schedule Variance) &nbsp;|&nbsp; CV = EV−AC (Cost Variance)</span><span class="formula">SPI = EV/PV (Schedule Performance Index) &nbsp;|&nbsp; CPI = EV/AC (Cost Performance Index)</span> SPI/CPI &lt; 1.0 = behind schedule / over budget. Forecasts: <span class="formula">EAC = BAC/CPI (Estimate at Completion) &nbsp;|&nbsp; ETC = EAC−AC</span> EVM is required on UK government contracts &gt;£2m and US federal contracts. It provides objective performance data rather than subjective % complete estimates — enabling early warning of cost/schedule overruns.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="management" data-q="What is a compensation event in NEC contracts?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is a Compensation Event in NEC contracts and how is it handled? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>Compensation Event (CE)</strong> in NEC is any event that is the Employer's risk and entitles the Contractor to a change in the Prices and/or Completion Date. Defined CEs (NEC4 Clause 60): Employer instructions, changes in law, unforeseen ground conditions, weather beyond 1-in-10-year threshold, Employer's failure to provide access/info, etc. Process: (1) Notification of CE within 8 weeks (or right is lost). (2) PM instructs quotation. (3) Contractor submits quotation (cost + time) within 3 weeks. (4) PM accepts or responds within 2 weeks. Quotations are based on <strong>Defined Cost</strong> (SSCC) plus fee percentage. The NEC CE mechanism replaces traditional claims — it's forward-looking, time-barred, and collaborative, avoiding legacy disputes common in traditional contracts.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="management" data-q="What is BIM and why is it important in civil engineering?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is BIM (Building Information Modelling) and what are the levels? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>BIM</strong> is a process of creating and managing digital information about the built environment — a 3D model containing geometric, physical, functional, and schedule data about every asset. <strong>BIM Levels:</strong> Level 0 — 2D CAD, no collaboration. Level 1 — 3D CAD, limited sharing. Level 2 — Federated models exchanged in common formats (IFC, COBie) — UK government mandate since 2016. Level 3 (iBIM) — Single integrated model, real-time collaboration. <strong>BIM dimensions:</strong> 3D (geometry), 4D (+time/programme), 5D (+cost), 6D (+sustainability), 7D (+FM data). Benefits: clash detection (Navisworks), reduced RFIs, construction sequencing, quantity takeoff, asset management. Common Data Environment (CDE) — shared platform (e.g., Viewpoint, BIM 360). ISO 19650 is the international standard for BIM information management.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── GENERAL ── -->
  <div class="iv-section" data-cat="general">
    <div class="iv-sec-title">💼 General Civil Engineering &amp; Career Questions</div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="Why did you choose civil engineering?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Why did you choose civil engineering as a career? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">This is a personal question — tailor your answer, but strong responses typically include: passion for visible, tangible impact on society and communities; interest in combining analytical problem-solving with creativity; attraction to diverse work — no two projects are the same; desire to work on infrastructure that serves future generations (bridges, roads, water systems). Mention specific formative experiences: a project/structure that inspired you, work experience, your degree specialisation, or the engineer who influenced you. Avoid generic answers — interviewers want genuine motivation. If possible, link your answer to the specific role: "I'm particularly interested in geotechnical work because..." Demonstrate both technical interest and awareness of the broader social responsibility of engineering.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What are the stages of the RIBA/RICS project stages?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are the RIBA Plan of Work stages? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">The RIBA Plan of Work 2020 provides a framework for building projects from inception to completion: <strong>Stage 0 — Strategic Definition:</strong> Business case, feasibility, project brief. <strong>Stage 1 — Preparation &amp; Briefing:</strong> Project requirements, site assessment, initial consultants. <strong>Stage 2 — Concept Design:</strong> Architectural concept, structural strategy, services concept. <strong>Stage 3 — Spatial Coordination:</strong> Coordinated design, planning application, cost plan. <strong>Stage 4 — Technical Design:</strong> Full production information, detailed engineering design, tender documentation. <strong>Stage 5 — Manufacturing &amp; Construction:</strong> Contractor procurement and construction. <strong>Stage 6 — Handover:</strong> Commissioning, golden thread documentation, O&amp;M manuals. <strong>Stage 7 — Use:</strong> Post-occupancy evaluation, FM, sustainability performance. Civil/infrastructure projects use similar stages aligned to NEC/RIBA or Infrastructure Client Group guidance.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What is the difference between a chartered and graduate engineer?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between a Graduate Engineer (GradEng) and Chartered Engineer (CEng)? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">A <strong>Graduate Engineer</strong> has completed an accredited degree (BEng/MEng) and may have registered with their professional body (e.g., ICE as AMICE, IStructE as AMIStructE). They are working towards chartership under a Training Agreement (MICE/CEng). A <strong>Chartered Engineer (CEng)</strong> is the highest professional qualification — demonstrates engineering competence, independence, management ability, and commitment to CPD. For ICE: submit Initial Professional Development (IPD) record, pass the Professional Review interview demonstrating UK-SPEC competencies. Required experience typically 3–5 years post-graduation. CEng brings: professional recognition, higher salary, signing authority on design/reports, eligibility for senior roles, international recognition. Incorporated Engineer (IEng) is an intermediate level between GradEng and CEng.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="Describe a challenging project or situation you encountered.">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Describe a challenging project or engineering situation you encountered. <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Use the <strong>STAR framework</strong>: Situation → Task → Action → Result. Example structure: "During my placement on [project type], we discovered [problem — e.g., unexpected ground conditions, design clash, programme risk]. As the responsible engineer, I had to [Task — e.g., assess options, brief the senior team]. I [Actions — e.g., conducted additional investigation, prepared cost-time comparison of options, liaised with client]. As a result, we [Result — delivered on time/budget, learned lesson, implemented new process]." Key tips: choose a genuinely technical challenge, not a generic interpersonal one; demonstrate engineering judgement, not just process-following; quantify outcomes if possible; show what you learned. This question tests initiative, problem-solving, and real-world competence beyond academic knowledge.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What are the key differences between the roles of designer, contractor and client in civil engineering?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">Explain the roles of the designer, contractor, and client in a civil engineering project. <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Client:</strong> Commissions the project, defines requirements, provides funding. Responsible for defining the brief, appointing the design team and contractor, and ultimately owning/operating the asset. Carries the risk of scope change and late information under NEC/JCT. <strong>Designer (Consultant/Engineer):</strong> Translates client requirements into buildable technical solutions. Responsible for structural/geotechnical/hydraulic design, specifications, drawings, and checking. Principal Designer role under CDM 2015. Fee-based appointment, professional indemnity insurance. <strong>Contractor:</strong> Constructs the designed works. Responsible for temporary works design (falsework, cofferdams, scaffolding), programme, health and safety on site, quality control, and NEC Project Manager obligations. Bears construction risk. Interface between these three parties — scope, information, risk, and programme — is the primary source of disputes on projects. Good project management aligns their interests through collaborative contracts and clear communication.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What is sustainability in civil engineering?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What does sustainability mean in civil engineering practice? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Sustainability in civil engineering encompasses environmental, social, and economic dimensions over a project's whole life: <strong>Environmental:</strong> Reducing embodied and operational carbon (steel and cement are major sources); minimising waste through circular economy principles; protecting biodiversity (BNG — Biodiversity Net Gain requirements in UK); sustainable drainage (SUDS); resilience to climate change (flood risk, heat). <strong>Social:</strong> Community consultation, inclusive design (accessibility), local employment, public realm improvement, safety. <strong>Economic:</strong> Whole-life cost rather than capital cost optimisation; asset longevity; maintenance planning. Tools: CEEQUAL (now ISC Envision, for infrastructure), BREEAM (buildings), embodied carbon assessment (ICE Carbon Database), Net Zero Carbon commitments. In interviews, reference specific initiatives: using GGBS/recycled aggregates, designing for disassembly, targeting Scope 1/2/3 carbon reduction, and referencing the UN SDGs or government Net Zero targets (2050 UK).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What are the main causes of bridge failure?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What are the main causes of bridge failures in history? <span class="iv-tag inter">Intermediate</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Historical bridge failures illuminate key engineering principles: <strong>Resonance/Dynamic effects:</strong> Tacoma Narrows (1940) — wind-induced aeroelastic flutter, not resonance as often stated. Lesson: aerodynamic analysis essential. <strong>Fatigue:</strong> Silver Bridge (1967) — stress-corrosion cracking in eyebar chain. Lesson: inspection programmes, fracture-critical member designation. <strong>Scour:</strong> Most common cause of bridge failure globally — flood scour undermines foundations. Lesson: scour protection, inspection after floods. <strong>Overloading:</strong> Design load assumptions exceeded. <strong>Corrosion:</strong> Inadequate cover, deicing salt, marine environment. <strong>Construction errors:</strong> Quebec Bridge (1907) — inadequate compression chord design during erection. <strong>Design error:</strong> I-35W Minneapolis (2007) — undersized gusset plates, accumulated DL. Lesson from all: importance of robust design, regular inspection, load monitoring, and conservative assumptions for fracture-critical elements.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What is the difference between serviceability and ultimate limit states?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What is the difference between ULS and SLS in structural design? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a"><strong>Ultimate Limit State (ULS)</strong> relates to structural collapse or major failure — loss of static equilibrium, excessive deformation, rupture, instability (buckling), fatigue failure. Designed using factored loads: <span class="formula">γG·Gk + γQ·Qk (e.g., 1.35DL + 1.5LL per EC0)</span> <strong>Serviceability Limit State (SLS)</strong> relates to conditions under which the structure becomes unfit for its intended use without collapsing — excessive deflection (span/250 for finishes, span/360 for brittle finishes), vibration, cracking (w_max = 0.3mm for RC), stress limits in prestressed concrete. SLS uses characteristic (unfactored or lightly factored) loads. In practice: ULS governs member sizing (strength); SLS governs detailing (reinforcement spacing for crack control, pre-stress levels, camber). Both must be satisfied for a code-compliant design.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="iv-item" data-cat="general" data-q="What are soft skills important for a civil engineer?">
      <div class="iv-q" onclick="ivToggle(this)"><span class="iv-q-text">What soft skills are most important for a civil engineer? <span class="iv-tag">Basic</span></span><span class="iv-arrow">▼</span></div>
      <div class="iv-a">Beyond technical competence, the most valued soft skills are: <strong>Communication:</strong> Explaining complex technical concepts clearly to non-engineers (clients, public, planners), writing clear reports/specifications, chairing meetings. <strong>Teamwork:</strong> Civil engineering is multidisciplinary — working with architects, M&amp;E engineers, contractors, surveyors. Understanding others' perspectives and constraints. <strong>Problem-solving:</strong> Engineering is fundamentally about resolving conflicts between constraints (cost, time, safety, programme, environment). <strong>Project management:</strong> Delivering on time and within budget — programme awareness, risk management, budget control. <strong>Commercial awareness:</strong> Understanding client objectives, contract implications, and cost impact of design decisions. <strong>Leadership &amp; initiative:</strong> Particularly as you become more senior — making decisions under uncertainty, mentoring junior staff. <strong>Resilience:</strong> Construction is challenging — dealing with unexpected problems, difficult stakeholders, and time pressure without compromising quality or safety.</div>
    </div>
